{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_howto-release-django","statement":"There are three types of Django releases: security releases, regular version releases, and pre-releases.","source_span":"There are three types of releases that you might need to make: * Security releases... * Regular version releases... * Pre-releases"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_howto-release-django","statement":"Security releases generally involve two or three simultaneous releases, e.g. 3.2.x, 4.0.x, and perhaps 4.1.x.","source_span":"This'll generally involve two or three simultaneous releases -- e.g. 3.2.x, 4.0.x, and, depending on timing, perhaps a 4.1.x."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_howto-release-django","statement":"A regular version release can be either a final release (e.g. 4.1) or a bugfix update (e.g. 4.1.1).","source_span":"either a final release (e.g. 4.1) or a bugfix update (e.g. 4.1.1)"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_howto-release-django","statement":"Pre-releases include versions such as 4.2 alpha, beta, or rc.","source_span":"Pre-releases: e.g. 4.2 alpha, beta, or rc."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_howto-release-django","statement":"For a security release, the security distribution list must be pre-notified one week before the actual release.","source_span":"If this is a security release, pre-notify the security distribution list one week before the actual release."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_howto-release-django","statement":"Required Unix tools for releasing Django include bash 4.0+, git, GPG, make, man, hashing tools, and python.","source_span":"bash (version 4.0+) * git * GPG * make * man * hashing tools... * python"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_howto-release-django","statement":"Building Django release artifacts requires a clean Python virtual environment with Python 3.10+ and pip 26.1+.","source_span":"A clean Python virtual environment (Python 3.10+, pip 26.1+) to build artifacts."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_howto-release-django","statement":"PyPI accounts used for Django releases must use only WebAuthn-based authentication factors, not TOTP one-time codes.","source_span":"Ensure your PyPI account only uses WebAuthn-based authentication factors, not TOTP (one-time codes)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_howto-release-django","statement":"Access to Django's project on Transifex requires a Manager role.","source_span":"Access to Django's project on Transifex... with a Manager role."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_howto-release-django","statement":"CVE IDs should be reserved 10 or more days before a security release.","source_span":"10 (or more) days before a security release"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_howto-release-django","statement":"Pre-notification of a security release must be sent out exactly one week before the release.","source_span":"Send out pre-notification exactly **one week** before the security release."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_howto-release-django","statement":"The security pre-notification email is signed with a command such as gpg --clearsign --digest-algo SHA256.","source_span":"$ gpg --clearsign --digest-algo SHA256 prenotification-email.txt"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_howto-release-django","fact_refs":[0,1,2,3],"question":"What are the three types of releases described in Django's release process, with examples?","answer":"Security releases (e.g. simultaneous 3.2.x/4.0.x/4.1.x fixes), regular version releases (a final release like 4.1 or a bugfix update like 4.1.1), and pre-releases (e.g. 4.2 alpha, beta, or rc)."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_howto-release-django","fact_refs":[4,10],"question":"How far in advance must Django's security distribution list be notified before a security release?","answer":"Exactly one week before the actual security release."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_howto-release-django","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What Python and pip versions are required for the virtual environment used to build Django release artifacts?","answer":"Python 3.10+ and pip 26.1+."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_howto-release-django","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What authentication requirement applies to PyPI accounts used for Django releases?","answer":"The PyPI account must use only WebAuthn-based authentication factors; TOTP one-time codes are not allowed."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_howto-release-django","fact_refs":[9],"question":"How many days before a Django security release should CVE IDs be reserved?","answer":"At least 10 (or more) days before the security release."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_howto-release-django","fact_refs":[11],"question":"What command is used to sign the Django security pre-notification email?","answer":"gpg --clearsign --digest-algo SHA256 prenotification-email.txt"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_howto-release-django","fact_refs":[8],"question":"What role is required for access to Django's project on Transifex?","answer":"A Manager role."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_mailing-lists","statement":"Security issues should be reported only to security@djangoproject.com, a private list open only to long-time, highly trusted Django developers, whose archives are not public.","source_span":"Please report security issues **only** to ``security@djangoproject.com``. This is a private list only open to long-time, highly trusted Django developers, and its archives are not public."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_mailing-lists","statement":"The Django Forum's \"Using Django\" category is for questions on installation, usage, or debugging of Django, and \"Internals\" is for discussion of Django's own development.","source_span":"Using Django_: to ask any question regarding the installation, usage, or debugging of Django. * Internals_: for discussion of the development of Django itself."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_mailing-lists","statement":"django-announce is a very low-traffic mailing list for announcing upcoming security releases, new Django releases, and security advisories.","source_span":"A (very) low-traffic list for announcing upcoming security releases, new releases of Django, and security advisories."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_mailing-lists","statement":"All ticket updates are mailed automatically to the django-updates mailing list, which is tracked by developers and interested community members.","source_span":"All the ticket updates are mailed automatically to this list, which is tracked by developers and interested community members."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_mailing-lists","statement":"The django-users and django-developers mailing lists are archived and no longer active, but remain available as a historical resource.","source_span":"The following mailing lists are archived and no longer active. These are still available as a historical resource."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_mailing-lists","statement":"The Using Django Forum category is now the preferred venue for usage questions, replacing the django-users mailing list.","source_span":"The Using Django_ category of the official Forum_ is now the preferred venue for asking usage questions."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_mailing-lists","statement":"The Internals Forum category is now the preferred venue for discussing Django's development, replacing the django-developers mailing list.","source_span":"The Internals_ category of the official Forum_ is now the preferred venue for discussing the development of Django."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_mailing-lists","fact_refs":[0],"question":"Where should Django security issues be reported, and who can access that list's archives?","answer":"They should be reported only to security@djangoproject.com, a private list restricted to long-time, highly trusted Django developers; its archives are not public."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_mailing-lists","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What is the django-announce mailing list used for?","answer":"It's a very low-traffic list for announcing upcoming security releases, new releases of Django, and security advisories."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_mailing-lists","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What gets posted automatically to the django-updates mailing list, and who follows it?","answer":"All ticket updates are mailed automatically to django-updates, which is tracked by developers and interested community members."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_mailing-lists","fact_refs":[1,5],"question":"Which Django Forum category should be used for questions about installing, using, or debugging Django, and what mailing list did it replace?","answer":"The \"Using Django\" category, which is now the preferred venue in place of the archived django-users mailing list."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_mailing-lists","fact_refs":[4,5,6],"question":"What happened to the django-developers and django-users mailing lists?","answer":"Both are archived and no longer active, kept only as historical resources; their discussions moved to the Forum's \"Internals\" and \"Using Django\" categories respectively."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_mailing-lists","fact_refs":[6],"question":"Which Django Forum category is now preferred for discussing Django's internal development?","answer":"The \"Internals\" category."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_organization","statement":"The Django Project is managed by a team of volunteers pursuing three goals: driving development of the Django framework, fostering the Django ecosystem, and leading the Django community per its Code of Conduct.","source_span":"managed by a team of volunteers pursuing three goals: - Driving the development of the Django web framework, - Fostering the ecosystem of Django-related software, - Leading the Django community in accordance with the values described in the Django Code of Conduct."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_organization","statement":"The Django Project is not a legal entity; the Django Software Foundation, a non-profit organization, handles its financial and legal matters.","source_span":"The Django Project isn't a legal entity. The Django Software Foundation, a non-profit organization, handles financial and legal matters related to the Django Project."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_organization","statement":"Mergers are a small set of people who merge pull requests to the Django Git repository.","source_span":"Mergers_ are a small set of people who merge pull requests to the Django Git repository."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_organization","statement":"A Merger must not merge a change primarily authored by that Merger unless it has been approved by another Merger, a steering council member, a triage & review team member, or a security team member.","source_span":"A Merger must not merge a change primarily authored by that Merger, unless the pull request has been approved by: - another Merger, - a steering council member, - a member of the triage & review team, or - a member of the security team."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_organization","statement":"The steering council selects Mergers as necessary to maintain a minimum of three, with no upper limit on the number of Mergers.","source_span":"The steering council_ selects Mergers_ as necessary to maintain their number at a minimum of three... There is no upper limit to the number of Mergers."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_organization","statement":"A person must not simultaneously serve as both a Merger and a steering council member; if a Merger is elected to the council, they immediately cease being a Merger.","source_span":"A person must not simultaneously serve as a member of the steering council. If a Merger is elected to the steering council, they shall cease to be a Merger immediately upon taking up membership in the steering council."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_organization","statement":"A person may serve in the roles of Releaser and Merger simultaneously.","source_span":"A person may serve in the roles of Releaser and Merger simultaneously."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_organization","statement":"Releasers are a small set of people who have the authority to upload packaged Django releases to the Python Package Index (PyPI) and to the djangoproject.com website.","source_span":"Releasers_ are a small set of people who have the authority to upload packaged releases of Django to the :pypi:`Python Package Index <Django>` and to the `djangoproject.com`_ website."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_organization","statement":"The steering council selects Releasers as necessary to maintain a minimum of three, with no upper limit on the number of Releasers.","source_span":"The steering council_ selects Releasers_ as necessary to maintain their number at a minimum of three... There is no upper limit to the number of Releasers."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_organization","statement":"The steering council provides oversight of Django's development and release process, assists in setting the direction of feature development and releases, selects Mergers and Releasers, and has a tie-breaking vote when other decision-making processes fail.","source_span":"The steering council is a group of experienced contributors who: - provide oversight of Django's development and release process, - assist in setting the direction of feature development and releases, - select Mergers and Releasers, and - have a tie-breaking vote when other decision-making processes fail."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_organization","fact_refs":[0],"question":"What are the three goals pursued by the volunteer team managing the Django Project?","answer":"Driving development of the Django web framework, fostering the Django-related software ecosystem, and leading the Django community in accordance with the Django Code of Conduct."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_organization","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What is the relationship between the Django Project and the Django Software Foundation?","answer":"The Django Project is not a legal entity itself; the Django Software Foundation, a non-profit, handles its financial and legal matters while the Project manages development, ecosystem, and community."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_organization","fact_refs":[3],"question":"Under what conditions can a Merger merge a pull request that they primarily authored themselves?","answer":"Only if it has been approved by another Merger, a steering council member, a triage & review team member, or a security team member."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_organization","fact_refs":[4,7],"question":"What is the minimum number of Mergers and Releasers the steering council must maintain, and is there a maximum for either?","answer":"A minimum of three each for Mergers and Releasers, with no upper limit on either role."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_organization","fact_refs":[5],"question":"Can someone serve as both a Merger and a member of the steering council at the same time?","answer":"No — if a Merger is elected to the steering council, they immediately cease being a Merger upon taking up that membership."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_organization","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What authority do Releasers have in the Django Project?","answer":"They have authority to upload packaged Django releases to the Python Package Index (PyPI) and to the djangoproject.com website."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_organization","fact_refs":[6],"question":"Can one person serve as both a Releaser and a Merger simultaneously in the Django Project?","answer":"Yes, a person may serve in both the Releaser and Merger roles at the same time."}
